## The Grade Jump Formula

This is the second hidden spec. It explains why G VS1 costs 10% more than H VS2 — and whether that matters.

At 0.75ct natural, here's what the major grade jumps cost:

| Grade jump | Price difference | Visual difference |
|---|---|---|
| H VS2 → G VS1 | +$74 ($1,888 vs $1,814 est.) | G is noticeably whiter face-up under bright light |
| G VS1 → F VS1 | +$300 approx | F is colorless; visible only in side-by-side comparison |
| F VS1 → F IF | +~$33 ($1,888 vs $1,921) | IF is flawless under 10× loupe; eye-clean in both |
| **What this means** | G→F is expensive for marginal visual gain | VS2→VS1 is cheap; VS1→IF is also cheap | Spend on color, not clarity upgrades past VS1 |

The insight: at 0.75ct, the jump from VS2 to VS1 costs almost nothing. The jump from VS1 to IF also costs almost nothing ($33 in current inventory). The jump from H to G costs about $74. The jump from G to F costs $300+.

So the grade formula: buy G or F color. Buy VS1 or IF clarity. Skip the expensive H→G→F color escalation. Go straight to F and stop.

This works because of how [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) prices map to visual impact. The jump from H to G gives you noticeably whiter face-up performance. The jump from G to F gives you the colorless designation. Almost no visible difference in real-world wear. The jump from F to E costs more for zero visible benefit at 0.75ct.

Clarity follows the same pattern. VS2 to VS1 costs almost nothing. VS1 to IF costs $33 in current inventory. IF to FL (flawless) is where price jumps sharply. Go to IF, stop there, redirect savings toward the setting or color grade.

Combo 4 shows this directly.

