## The Lab Size Wall

This is the third hidden spec. It explains why lab and natural diverge so dramatically at $5K.

At $5,000, a natural 1ct marquise costs $4,049–$4,629 depending on grade. That leaves $371–$951 for a setting. You're stone-heavy, setting-light. Natural 0.75ct gives more setting budget but limits visual size.

Lab at $5,000 works differently. A 1.5ct D IF lab costs $2,682. That leaves $2,318 for a setting. A 1.5ct D VVS2 costs $2,493. A 1.25ct D VVS1 costs $1,222 — leaving $3,778 for a setting. If you're going lab, you hit a size wall not from budget, but from availability.

| Lab carat size | Price (D, top clarity) | Setting budget left | Ring total range |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1ct D VVS1 | $958 | $4,042 | Most budget is setting |
| 1.25ct D VVS1 | $1,222 | $3,778 | Plenty for any setting |
| 1.5ct D IF | $2,682 | $2,318 | Matches premium settings |
| 1.5ct D VVS2 | $2,493 | $2,507 | Plenty for side stone |
| **What this means** | Lab cost rises slowly | Size isn't the constraint | Grade is the differentiator at $5K |

The lab size wall kicks in around 2ct, where prices jump more sharply. Under $5K, 1.5ct D IF or D VVS2 is achievable. Compare with the [lab-grown marquise diamond](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond) guide for the full size-to-price breakdown.

For everything on resale and long-term value between lab and natural, read [Marquise Natural vs Lab](/marquise-natural-vs-lab).

