## The Setting Budget Flip

At $3,000, most of your budget goes to the setting. The stone is lab at 0.50–0.75ct and cheap. The setting carries 67–78% of the spend. That's the right call. The setting adds visual value the stone alone can't provide.

At $5,000, the math flips. Combo 1 puts 83% on the stone ($4,049 of $4,909). Combo 3 puts 45% on the stone ($1,888 of $4,238). The [marquise cut diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) earns its place as a stone-first shape at $5K. The stone is big enough to carry a simpler setting.

Here's the budget split across all five combos:

| Combo | Setting cost | Stone cost | Setting % | Stone % |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 — Solitaire WG | $860 | $4,049 | 17% | 83% |
| 2 — Three Stone YG | $1,970 | $2,682 | 42% | 58% |
| 3 — Side Stone WG | $2,350 | $1,888 | 55% | 45% |
| 4 — Floral 18K WG | $2,210 | $1,921 | 53% | 47% |
| 5 — Side Stone RG | $2,350 | $2,493 | 49% | 51% |
| **What this means** | Stone-heavy at 1ct natural | Balanced at 0.75ct | Setting-heavy only at sub-$3K | $5K = genuine choice |

Combo 1 is the extreme. You're spending $860 on the setting because $4,049 already went to the stone. That's the cost of buying a real GIA natural 1ct. The setting doesn't need to compete — the stone speaks for itself.

Combos 3 and 4 are more balanced. A $2,350 shared prong side stone setting earns its price by adding flanking diamonds. A $2,210 floral 18K setting earns it with petal-prong craftsmanship and 18K gold. Both add something the stone alone doesn't.

The flip point is around $2,500–$3,000 in stone value. Below that, spend on the setting. Above that, let the stone lead and choose a setting that supports it without competing. Every combo here lands on the right side of that line. For more on matching stone to setting style, see the [marquise solitaire](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring) and [marquise three stone](/marquise-three-stone-engagement-ring) guides.

