## How to Order Without Overpaying

Three things to check before you click buy.

**1. Watch the bow-tie in the 360° video.** The [full video check process](/how-to-buy-marquise-diamond) takes under two minutes. Every marquise has a bow-tie shadow across the width. It varies by cut. The [bow-tie effect](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie) on a 1ct natural looks different from a 0.75ct natural. Open the stone's video on the detail page. If the shadow covers more than 15% of the face-up width, find a different stone in the same grade range.

**2. Check the L/W ratio on the detail page.** The ideal marquise length-to-width ratio is 1.85–2.05. A ratio below 1.75 looks stubby. A ratio above 2.15 looks needle-thin. The [marquise diamond length to width ratio](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio) guide shows why this matters more than color or clarity for visual appeal.

**3. Confirm the stone is still available before choosing a setting.** Certified inventory moves. The stone IDs in this guide were live at time of writing. Always click the stone link first, confirm it's in stock, then go to the setting. If the stone is gone, the same search with the same filters will show current alternatives in the same grade range.

One more thing: resize costs money. Order half a size up if you're between sizes. It's easier to size down than up on a marquise prong setting. Most jewelers charge $30–$100 to resize. A solitaire can be resized by up to two sizes. A channel or pavé band is harder — check first.

